@namespace NSail.Components

@* What a menu says rather than offers: who you are at its top, the version at its foot. It
   is not an item — no click, no tab stop of its own; a field it holds keeps its own — and it is
   presentational to the list it renders inside, so a menu never offers an option that does
   nothing. The rule that separates it from the items is drawn by position (ns-mud.css), not
   written here: a block that opens the menu is ruled underneath and one that closes it is ruled
   above, so neither end needs a divider the caller has to remember. *@
@* Escape and Tab from a control the block holds (a menu's own search) close the menu: the
   vendor answers both on the list's wrapper only while the menu holds a row, and a menu that is
   a search alone holds none until something is typed. A Tab between two controls of one block
   closes it too, as the vendor's does whenever the menu holds a row, so one shape of menu does
   not answer the key two ways. A control that answers Escape itself (a lookup's open list) stops it
   before it gets here; one that stops Tab with it hands the Tab on through Dismiss, since a Tab
   out of the menu is never the inner list's alone. A menu with rows is closed by the vendor too,
   and a second close of a closed menu is absorbed. *@
<div class="ns-menu-block" role="presentation" @onkeydown="Dismiss">
    <CascadingValue Value="this" IsFixed="true">
        @ChildContent
    </CascadingValue>
</div>

@code {

    [CascadingParameter]
    MudMenu? Menu { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    internal Task Dismiss(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        if (Menu is null || args.Key is not ("Escape" or "Tab"))
        {
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }

        return Menu.CloseMenuAsync();
    }
}
